From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:14:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] ws397: PWR key on the AXP2101 PWRKEY, wake pin split
 from the power pin
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The ws397 has a separate PWR button wired to the PMIC PWRKEY input, not to a
GPIO, so OK stops being the power key (InputStyle::DigitalButtons) and the
profile grows two fields:

  InputPins.wakePin  the RTC-capable key that arms the deep-sleep wake when the
                     power key is not a GPIO (ws397: OK on GPIO5). Every other
                     board leaves it unassigned and keeps waking on `power`.
  BoardProfile.pmicIrq  the PMIC interrupt line (ws397: GPIO38, open-drain,
                     active-low, NOT an RTC GPIO — hence wakePin).

PowerManager::wakeSourcePin() exposes the choice, armWakeOnPins() now returns
whether it actually armed (it silently did nothing when a pin was not
RTC-capable on ext1 parts) and InputManager::isWakePinPhysicallyPressed()
verifies the post-wake hold on the right pin.

Also: the first paint after begin() is promoted out of the `!turnOff` branch in
Ssd1677Driver, and an explicit FULL is no longer downgraded to HALF.

